Frequently Asked Questions

Start with 5-10 lbs, ruck 2-3 times per week on level ground, and keep each session to 20-30 minutes. Hold that load for the first month, adding an extra weekly ruck or more distance before more weight. A good beginner target is 8-12 weekly miles with a moderate load; one brutal heavy session does less.

Changes in body composition usually show up in waist measurements and the weekly weight average after several weeks of a steady deficit, and they're easier to see by week 8-12. Early weeks often show little scale movement because new training brings some water retention, so circumference measurements and progress photos are better indicators than the scale alone.

Yes, as long as your diet keeps you in a deficit; progress is just slower. Three one-hour rucks at roughly 400 calories each add about 1,200 calories of activity a week, a little less once you subtract what you'd burn at rest. Three sessions you can sustain will do more than five you can't keep up.

A light carb 30-60 minutes before (banana, toast) provides energy without digestive issues, then a meal with protein and carbs afterwards for recovery. Rucking fasted isn't necessary for fat loss: over weeks, the total calorie deficit matters far more than whether you ate beforehand, so eat if it makes the session feel better.

Water retention from a new exercise stimulus and the inflammation that comes with muscle adaptation can mask fat loss on the scale early on. This is normal and usually settles within a few weeks as your body adapts to loaded walking.

Not with a loaded pack while you're building up. Four or five loaded sessions a week is plenty for most people, with at least one unloaded day between rucks. Unloaded walking on the other days still adds to the week's activity without stacking more load on tired feet and joints.

Reduce load by 5-10 pounds and check your shoes; the usual culprits are too much weight too soon or worn-out footwear. If pain persists beyond a few days of reduced load, take a full rest week, and get it checked if it doesn't settle. Pushing through joint pain under load tends to turn a small problem into a long layoff.

On flat, firm ground at the same speed and load, it's close enough to plan with; at walking pace, air resistance adds little. The real outdoor differences are terrain and hills. Soft or uneven ground raises the cost, which is what the calculator's terrain setting covers, and grade raises it most. If you want a treadmill session closer to a hilly route, add incline gradually: on the Pandolf numbers, a 5 percent grade takes a 180 lb person with 30 lbs at 3.5 mph from about 410 to about 635 calories an hour.

